WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.451
Tool execution controls are where MCP security becomes operational.

00:00:03.451 --> 00:00:06.430
Read-only tools and write-capable tools carry different risk.

00:00:06.430 --> 00:00:10.526
A read-only inventory query may expose sensitive data if poorly controlled,

00:00:10.526 --> 00:00:14.621
but a write-capable tool can modify files, send messages, create tickets,

00:00:14.621 --> 00:00:17.600
change cloud resources, update repositories, or trigger workflows.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:22.813
High-impact actions should never rely on the model alone to decide what is safe.

00:00:22.813 --> 00:00:26.909
Command execution, file modification, email, messaging, code repository changes, cloud actions,

00:00:26.909 --> 00:00:29.887
ticketing, and workflow automation all need external enforcement.

00:00:29.887 --> 00:00:33.238
The model can produce arguments or recommend an action,

00:00:33.238 --> 00:00:35.845
but validation should happen in deterministic code.

00:00:35.845 --> 00:00:38.079
Schema validation checks shape and type.

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:40.685
Argument validation checks allowed values and constraints.

00:00:40.685 --> 00:00:43.664
Allowlisted operations limit what the tool can do.

00:00:43.664 --> 00:00:48.504
Policy checks decide whether this user, in this context, can request this operation.

00:00:48.504 --> 00:00:52.600
Transaction previews and dry-run modes help users understand consequences before execution.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:57.441
A preview can show what file will change, which ticket will be updated,

00:00:57.441 --> 00:01:01.536
who will receive a message, what cloud resource will be modified,

00:01:01.536 --> 00:01:03.770
or which repository branch is affected.

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:07.494
Human approval gates are especially important for irreversible, costly, external,

00:01:07.494 --> 00:01:08.611
or privileged actions.

00:01:08.611 --> 00:01:12.334
Approval should be tied to the specific action and arguments,

00:01:12.334 --> 00:01:15.313
not just to a generic permission granted earlier.

00:01:15.313 --> 00:01:17.547
Rollback planning completes the control story.

00:01:17.547 --> 00:01:19.781
If a tool can make changes,

00:01:19.781 --> 00:01:23.505
the team should know how to undo or contain mistakes.

00:01:23.505 --> 00:01:27.973
That may involve version history, reversible workflows, backups, change records, rate limits,

00:01:27.973 --> 00:01:29.462
or incident response procedures.

00:01:29.462 --> 00:01:34.675
Safe operations assume that models can misunderstand context and users can approve too quickly.

00:01:34.675 --> 00:01:40.260
The system should make the safe path easy and enforce hard limits outside the model.
